WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.666
Welcome to Module Nine, Lesson One: guardrails and the five rail types.

2
00:00:04.996 --> 00:00:08.210
Guardrails are runtime checks between your app and the model.

3
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:13.696
Picture PetPal, an online pet pharmacy whose support agent can issue refunds.

4
00:00:14.026 --> 00:00:16.152
In its first week, three things go wrong.

5
00:00:16.952 --> 00:00:19.462
It happily compares prices with a rival store.

6
00:00:19.792 --> 00:00:22.857
Then someone talks it into a nine-hundred-dollar refund.

7
00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:26.871
It even repeats a stranger's address from a retrieved note.

8
00:00:27.201 --> 00:00:29.945
The only control was a polite line in the prompt.

9
00:00:30.745 --> 00:00:33.362
Guardrails put checks at five points around the model.

10
00:00:33.692 --> 00:00:36.076
NeMo Guardrails calls them the input,

11
00:00:36.126 --> 00:00:39.167
dialog, retrieval, execution, and output rails.

12
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:43.416
The input rail runs before the model sees the user's message.

13
00:00:43.746 --> 00:00:47.579
It catches jailbreaks, redacts personal data, and restricts topics.

14
00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:51.529
The dialog rail keeps conversations on approved paths.

15
00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:54.612
The retrieval rail screens retrieved chunks for

16
00:00:54.662 --> 00:00:57.953
sensitive or injected text before they enter the prompt.

17
00:00:58.753 --> 00:01:01.327
The execution rail wraps every tool call.

18
00:01:01.657 --> 00:01:04.867
It restricts which tools may run, validates arguments,

19
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:07.644
and requires approval for high-impact actions.

20
00:01:08.444 --> 00:01:11.658
The output rail checks the response before the user sees it.

21
00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:16.738
It filters toxicity and personal data, checks grounding, and enforces format.

22
00:01:17.538 --> 00:01:20.891
So for PetPal, competitor questions hit the input rail,

23
00:01:20.941 --> 00:01:23.056
and big refunds the execution rail.

24
00:01:23.386 --> 00:01:27.667
The leaked address belongs on the retrieval rail, with output as a backstop.

25
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:32.790
Rails are written in Colang, as user intents, bot responses, and flows.

26
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.910
If you see define user or define flow, think NeMo Guardrails.

27
00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.284
But guardrails are one layer, not the whole answer.

28
00:01:40.614 --> 00:01:44.681
Defence in depth stacks independent controls, because any one can fail.

29
00:01:45.481 --> 00:01:47.928
For a sensitive action, name an input rail,

30
00:01:47.978 --> 00:01:51.469
server-side tool permissions, and approval on the risky step.

31
00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:53.861
Add an output rail and an audit record.

32
00:01:54.661 --> 00:01:57.704
Rails add latency, since some checks are model calls.

33
00:01:58.034 --> 00:02:00.986
Run cheap checks like regex and allow-lists first,

34
00:02:01.036 --> 00:02:03.147
and save model checks for judgement.

35
00:02:03.947 --> 00:02:08.057
On the exam, expect one requirement with all five rails as options.

36
00:02:08.387 --> 00:02:10.577
Ask where the check must run, then read on.
